## PART I. MERCHANT-GROUP COMMUNICATION (CHAT) AND CHAT MODERATION

Each group has a chat channel between the merchant who owns the product and the group, with the group's moderator as the group's main voice. Chat is the only way a merchant interacts with a group after creating it.

### Who can chat
- The merchant who owns the product can send messages to the group and receive replies.
- The group's moderator can message the merchant on behalf of the group and reply to the merchant.
- Other members of the group can read the chat of their own group. Whether they can also write is controlled by the moderator, with writing limited to the moderator by default. Flag this to me if the existing group logic suggests a different rule.
- Before a group has a moderator (nobody has invited anyone yet), the merchant can still notify the group, and members of that group receive it.
- Nobody outside the group, and no other merchant, can see or send messages in that chat. Enforce this on the backend.
- Chat is private to the group and the product's merchant. Chat messages are never public.

### Merchant notifications and the group link
- Every chat message that comes from a group appears in the merchant's private notifications (Part A), showing the group name, the product, a short preview of the message and the time.
- Each notification carries a link to the group where the message came from.
- When the merchant taps the link, they navigate directly to that group, which opens in VIEW-ONLY mode by default.
- From the view-only screen the merchant can choose to chat back: tap "Reply" to open the chat input, then respond. Until they tap reply, they only view.
- Replying in the chat resolves the related notification as handled. Unread chat notifications are counted and shown in the merchant's notification section.
- The same link-to-group behaviour works for group moderators: when the merchant sends a message or notice, the moderator and members receive a notification that links to the group chat.
- A merchant's view-only access to the group does not give them any authority. They see the group and the chat, never the members' private data (wallets, addresses, personal details).

### Chat must be positive: moderation by the platform
- Chat must remain respectful and positive. Insults, abuse, threats, harassment and defamatory language are not allowed, by anyone: merchants, moderators or members.
- The platform scans every message on the server BEFORE it is delivered. A message that breaks the rules is either censored (offending words masked) or blocked, depending on severity.
- The platform sends an automatic warning to the culprit explaining which rule was broken. The warning is delivered to the culprit's private notifications as a "platform warning".
- Repeat offenders are escalated by strikes: first violation is a warning, repeat violations lead to a temporary chat mute, and continued violations lead to the culprit being BLACKLISTED from chat by the platform. A blacklisted user cannot send chat messages. They can still read, and a clear notice explains the status and how to appeal.
- Blacklisting a merchant from chat must not remove their ability to receive notifications from groups, so group members can still reach them through the platform's normal notice flow. Flag this to me if you see a conflict.
- Defamatory language includes false and damaging statements about a person or business, and insults aimed at individuals. The system uses a combination of a configurable word and phrase list (including English, Kiswahili and Sheng) and an automated classifier, plus a "Report message" button for members to flag content. Admins review flagged and censored messages in the Chat Moderation section and can confirm, reverse, warn, mute or blacklist.
- Strike thresholds, mute durations and the word list are configurable by an admin and are not hard-coded.
- Every moderation action (censor, block, warning, mute, blacklist, appeal result) is written to the audit log with who, what, when, the original message (stored privately for admins only) and the reason.
- Moderation also protects the merchant: members cannot harass the merchant, and the same rules apply to everyone.

## BACKEND REQUIREMENTS (AI STUDIO MUST BUILD A POWERFUL BACKEND)

Build a robust, secure, scalable backend for all of the above, using the backend the project already uses (Firebase with Cloud Functions and Security Rules, or Supabase with Postgres, RLS and Edge Functions). Do not switch backends. Critical logic runs on the server only, never in the client.

### 1. Server-side business logic
- All sensitive actions go through server endpoints or functions, not direct client writes: subscribe, upgrade, pay with wallet coins, submit manual payment, approve or decline payment, grant exemption, record shop visit, verify or decline merchant, create product, feature product, create group, join group, invite, assign moderator, send chat message, report message, send notification, broadcast announcement, warn, mute and blacklist.
- Derive the user from the verified session or token. Never trust user IDs, plan names, prices, limits, exemptions or roles sent from the client.
- Validate every input on the server (types, ranges, required fields, file types and sizes). Return clear, consistent error messages and codes.

### 2. Merchant verification engine
- A merchant_verification record per merchant: status (pending review, visit scheduled, visit completed, verified, declined), the mandatory requirements and whether each is met, exempted or not met, and the shop visit details (scheduled date, representative, completed date, notes, photos, advert installed date and photo).
- Exemptions are a separate table: merchant_id, requirement (physical_shop, trading_licence, id_or_passport, three_years_operating, shop_visit, or all), reason (required, not empty), granted_by (admin), granted_at. Only admins can write to it, and every grant or revoke goes to the audit log.
- A verification guard function computes whether every non-exempted requirement is met and the shop visit and advert are done (or exempted). The merchant can be set to "verified" only when the guard passes. Reject otherwise.
- The product and group creation guard checks verification status "verified" in addition to the active subscription.
- The "Buyoll Verified Merchant" badge is derived from the verified status on the server, never set by the client.

### 3. Subscription and upgrade engine
- Plans table as the single source of truth for price, limits and benefits.
- Subscription records: merchant_id, plan_id, status (pending, active, expired, declined), start_date, end_date, payment_id.
- A guard function checks, on every product or group creation: merchant verified, subscription active and not expired, product count within the plan limit, groups per product within the plan limit (counting every group ever created, since groups are never deleted). Reject otherwise.
- An upgrade guard rejects any upgrade or plan-change payment while the current subscription is still active.
- A scheduled job (cron or scheduled function) runs at least hourly to: expire subscriptions at 30 days, end featured periods, send "expiring soon" notices (for example 3 days before and on the day), and update statuses.
- Plan activation is automatic after a successful wallet payment or an admin approval, and the new limits take effect immediately.

### 4. Wallet and payments ledger
- Use a double-entry style ledger: every transfer creates a debit entry and a credit entry with the same transaction ID.
- Wallet transfers run in a single atomic database transaction: debit merchant, credit admin wallet, record the ledger entries, update both balances, activate the subscription or featured period. If any step fails, everything rolls back.
- Idempotency keys on every payment request so a double tap or retry never charges twice.
- Block negative balances and block overdrafts with row locking or transaction checks.
- Manual payments are stored as pending with code, amount, reason, method (till or paybill), timestamp and merchant. Reject duplicate payment codes. Only admins can approve or decline, and approval is itself atomic and idempotent.
- Featured fee is computed on the server as KES 30 per day times the number of days chosen.
- Keep an immutable audit log for payments, approvals, plan changes, verification decisions, exemptions, shop visits, chat moderation actions and admin actions (who, what, when, before and after).

### 5. Groups and concurrency
- Group capacity enforced with a transaction or row lock, so two members joining at the same moment can never exceed the speed limit.
- Prevent the same member from joining the same group twice. Allow the same member to join many different groups.
- The first-inviter-becomes-moderator rule is decided on the server, atomically, so only one person can win it.
- Only the product's owner merchant can CREATE groups or upload the group icon at creation, checked on the server.
- Group update and delete operations are disabled for merchants at both the rules level and the function level. The merchant role has create-only permission on groups, then read-only. No endpoint exists for a merchant to edit or delete a group. Group changes (membership, capacity, moderator) happen only through server functions triggered by members' actions.
- Group speed and icon are immutable after creation (enforce with rules or database constraints and triggers).

### 6. Chat engine
- Collections or tables: chat_threads (one per group, linked to the group, product and merchant), chat_messages (thread_id, sender_id, sender_role, text, status [delivered, censored, blocked, removed], created_at, read_by), chat_reports, chat_strikes, chat_blacklist.
- Chat is real-time (Firebase listeners or Supabase Realtime), with pagination for history, delivery state and read state.
- Message send goes through a server function that: verifies the sender is the product's merchant, the group moderator, or an allowed member of that group; checks the sender is not muted or blacklisted; runs the moderation pipeline; stores the message; then triggers notifications.
- Moderation pipeline on the server: normalize text (case, spacing, character swaps), check against the configurable word and phrase list, run the classifier for insults, threats and defamatory language, and return one of: allow, censor (mask offending words) or block. The original text of censored and blocked messages is kept privately for admin review, never shown to recipients.
- Strike system: record each violation, send a warning notification from the platform to the culprit, apply escalation (warning, temporary mute, blacklist) according to admin-configured thresholds, and notify the user at each step. Blacklist and mute are enforced by the server on every send.
- Rate limiting and anti-spam on chat sends (per user, per thread).
- Merchant notification fan-out: when a message from a group is delivered, create a notification for the merchant containing group_id, product_id, message preview and a deep link to the group (view-only mode). Group-side notifications for messages from the merchant link to the group chat in the same way.
- Deep link behaviour: the link opens the group page for the merchant in view-only mode; the "Reply" action enables the chat input. Authorization is checked again on the server when the link opens, so a forged link to someone else's group shows nothing ("not found").
- Report message: members and merchants can report a message. Reports go to the admin Chat Moderation queue.
- Appeals: a blacklisted user can submit an appeal that an admin reviews.

### 7. Notifications engine
- Notifications are stored per user with type, title, body, link (deep link), read status and timestamp, readable only by the owner.
- Admin broadcast fans out to all users reliably (batched background job for large user counts, not one slow request), and new users also see active broadcasts.
- Trigger notifications automatically for verification updates (visit scheduled, visit completed, verified, exemption granted, declined), payment approved or declined, subscription activated, expiring or expired, plan upgraded, featured started or ended, group events, chat messages, chat warnings, mutes and blacklist notices, moderator messages and warnings.

### 8. Security and access control
- Row-level security or security rules on every collection or table: owner-only for notifications, profiles, wallets, merchant and lender data; group chat readable only by the group's members and the product's merchant. Public read only for announcements, featured products, products and their groups (public fields only), and the verified badge.
- Role checks (member, merchant, lender, admin) on the server for every admin or merchant endpoint.
- Return "not found" instead of forbidden when a user requests another user's resource.
- Rate limiting and abuse protection on payment, join, invite, chat and upload endpoints.
- Store uploaded trading licences, ID documents, shop visit photos and notes in private storage, accessible only to the owner and admins, with signed URLs. Only the verified badge is public.
- Never log sensitive data (document numbers, balances tied to names, full chat content) in plain logs.

### 9. Performance and reliability
- Add database indexes for the common queries (products by status, groups by product, notifications by user and date, chat messages by thread and date, subscriptions by merchant and end date, payments by status, merchants by verification status, chat reports by status).
- Paginate lists (products, groups, notifications, chat messages, admin queues).
- Use transactions for any multi-step write. Make background jobs retry-safe.
